t decode values twice, which would explain why `%2526` ends up as `&` instead of the encoded form. 2526` → `%26`. Then a second decode would give `&`.\n\nActually, I think the issue is simpler. `UrlQuerySanitizer` from Android decodes URL-encoded values. But `uri.getQuery()` returns the raw query string (not decoded). When you pass it to `parseQuery`:\n\n1. `parseQuery` splits on `&` and `=`\n2. Then it URL-decodes each part\n\nFor `q=a%26b%3Dc`:\n- Split on `&` - there are none (it's all one param since `%26` isn't a literal `&`)\n- Value is `a%26b%3Dc`\n- Decode it: `a&b=c`\n\nThat should give us one parameter with value `a&b=c`. But the bug shows the value is split into separate parameters `q=a` and `b=c`, which means something is decoding the query string first before splitting, turning `%26` into `&` and then splitting on that literal `&`. The `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` approach was meant to prevent double-decoding, but it's too aggressive and breaks things by encoding the `%` signs themselves.\n\nThe fix is to escape only the percent signs that are part of valid percent-encoded sequences. I can use regex to find `%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}` patterns and replace just the `%` with `%25`, so `%26` becomes `%2526`. This way the sanitizer will decode `%2526` to `%26` and the value sanitizer can handle it correctly without prematurely splitting on decoded characters.\n\nLet me trace through the flow: with `%26` becoming `%2526`, `parseQuery` splits on literal `&` characters (none exist), gives us parameter `q` with encoded value `a%2526b%253Dc`, then decoding produces `a%26b%3Dc`, and finally the value sanitizer receives this double-encoded string.\n\nBut that's not what we want either—we need the final decoded value to be `a&b=c`, not `a%26b%3Dc`. So this approach is fundamentally flawed because we're preventing the sanitizer from ever decoding the value.\n\nThe core issue is that `Uri.getQuery()` returns the raw query string, and `parseQuery` splits on `&` and `=` before decoding. If the query contains percent-encoded delimiters like `%26`, they're not split on immediately, but if the query gets decoded first, those encoded characters become real delimiters and break the parsing.\n\nThe double-decoding comment suggests that simply passing the raw query might cause `parseQuery` to decode values twice, which is why the `%` replacement was attempted—but that solution breaks legitimate encoded parameters. I need to find a way to handle both cases correctly. I need to reconsider the approach. The `.replace(\"%\", \"%25\")` fix prevents the initial split from treating `%26` as a literal `&`, but then the sanitizer decodes `%2526` to `%26` without further decoding to `&`. \n\nThe real issue is that `UrlQuerySanitizer` appears to perform multiple decode passes—once on the raw query string which splits on literal `&` and `=`, and again on each value. The solution isn't to blanket-replace all percent signs, but rather to be more surgical about which ones to escape so the decoder behaves correctly. I'm realizing that replacing `%` with `%25` before decoding would work—`%26` becomes `%2526`, which decodes to `&`, and `%3D` becomes `%253D`, which decodes to `=`. So the approach of escaping percent signs first should preserve the encoded characters through the decoding process.\n\nBut let me reconsider the actual flow: `Uri.getQuery()` returns the raw query string without decoding, so `parseQuery()` receives the already-encoded string. The issue is that `parseQuery()` is performing double-decoding—first when splitting the query parameters, then again when decoding the values themselves.\n\nTo prevent this, I should use a regex pattern that only escapes percent signs that are part of valid percent-encoded sequences like `%26` or `%3D`, replacing just the `%` with `%25` to become `%2526` and `%253D`.\n\nNow I'm implementing this fix by finding all percent-encoded sequences and escaping them before parsing the query string.\n\nHowever, I need to reconsider whether this approach handles all edge cases correctly. If the query string contains a malformed percent sequence like `%2g`, my regex won't match it, so it won't be escaped and `UrlQuerySanitizer` might decode it unpredictably. I'm also thinking about how to properly distinguish between percent signs that are part of valid encoding sequences versus standalone percent signs.\n\nFor the actual use case—parsing query strings for display—I should handle percent-encoded values by preventing them from being decoded during parameter splitting, then apply the value sanitizer afterward to preserve the encoded representation. But I'm realizing that simply encoding `%` signs won't work as expected, since the sanitizer still decodes the values at the end, which means the encoding would be lost.\n\nLet me reconsider the actual behavior: without any special handling, if the query is `q=a%26b%3Dc`, the parser would split on the decoded `&` character, incorrectly treating it as a parameter separator rather than part of the value. This suggests I need a different approach to preserve the original encoding through the parsing process. The regex `(?i)%[0-9a-f]{2}` correctly identifies percent-encoded sequences like `%26` and `%3D`. When these are replaced with their `%25` equivalents—`%2526` and `%253D`—and then passed through URL decoding, they become `%26` and `%3D`, which preserve the original encoded values for display. This approach prevents the double-decoding issue by escaping the percent signs before decoding occurs. Now I need to implement this fix, but first I should examine the existing test cases to understand what behavior is actually expected.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
